WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.899 --> 00:00:09.165 line:2% position:50%,center size:96% align:center
Welcome to New Zealand's regions, part 1 of 1. First, I clear the chosen names
for a fresh regional lesson.

2
00:00:09.819 --> 00:00:21.801 line:2% position:50%,center size:96% align:center
We work through 16 of the New Zealand regions the map names, once each: where
it sits, one fact worth keeping, and a hook to remember it by.

3
00:00:22.473 --> 00:00:24.644 line:2% position:50%,center size:96% align:center
Let's bring Northland into view.

4
00:00:29.680 --> 00:00:42.045 line:2% position:50%,center size:96% align:center
Northland fills the far north of the North Island, reaching out to Three Kings
Bay, with its largest city, Whangarei, on the east coast and the Auckland
region just below it.

5
00:00:42.702 --> 00:00:57.030 line:2% position:50%,center size:96% align:center
The Treaty of Waitangi, which set out how the country would be shared between
Māori and the British Crown, was signed at Waitangi in 1840, and every year
the treaty flag is raised on the marae at the bay.

6
00:00:57.685 --> 00:01:05.440 line:2% position:50%,center size:96% align:center
Remember the far north with the treaty grounds at the bay and the giant kauri
standing in the Waipoua forest.

7
00:01:06.176 --> 00:01:08.301 line:2% position:50%,center size:96% align:center
Let's bring Auckland into view.

8
00:01:13.553 --> 00:01:25.790 line:2% position:50%,center size:96% align:center
Auckland sits on the narrow neck of land in the north-east of the North
Island, where its largest city straddles two harbours, with Northland to the
north and Waikato to the south.

9
00:01:26.451 --> 00:01:35.495 line:2% position:50%,center size:96% align:center
The city is built over the vents of about fifty small volcanoes, the highest
of them Mount Eden, and it is the largest city in the country.

10
00:01:36.151 --> 00:01:42.838 line:2% position:50%,center size:96% align:center
Remember the harbour city on a field of volcanoes, named for an English earl
who never came here.

11
00:01:43.543 --> 00:01:45.888 line:2% position:50%,center size:96% align:center
Let's bring Waikato into view.

12
00:01:51.206 --> 00:02:04.859 line:2% position:50%,center size:96% align:center
Waikato is the large inland region in the middle of the North Island, running
from the Auckland border down to Taranaki and Hawke's Bay, with its largest
city, Hamilton, in the centre.

13
00:02:05.518 --> 00:02:13.784 line:2% position:50%,center size:96% align:center
The Shire from The Lord of the Rings was built for the film on a farm near
Matamata, and the set was rebuilt in permanent timber and kept open.

14
00:02:14.446 --> 00:02:23.235 line:2% position:50%,center size:96% align:center
Remember the flowing water that names the region, the longest river in the
country, and the caves lit by thousands of glowworms.

15
00:02:23.960 --> 00:02:26.386 line:2% position:50%,center size:96% align:center
Let's bring Bay of Plenty into view.

16
00:02:33.023 --> 00:02:45.516 line:2% position:50%,center size:96% align:center
Bay of Plenty runs along the east coast of the North Island, north of Hawke's
Bay, with its largest city, Tauranga, on the shore and Gisborne at its far
eastern corner.

17
00:02:46.172 --> 00:02:55.936 line:2% position:50%,center size:96% align:center
Te Puke calls itself the kiwifruit capital of the world, and most of the
kiwifruit shipped overseas is grown in this bay.

18
00:02:56.600 --> 00:03:05.133 line:2% position:50%,center size:96% align:center
Remember the bay named for a feast, with the kiwifruit town inland and a
smoking volcano out at sea.

19
00:03:05.859 --> 00:03:07.775 line:2% position:50%,center size:96% align:center
Let's bring Gisborne into view.

20
00:03:14.370 --> 00:03:24.773 line:2% position:50%,center size:96% align:center
Gisborne is the far north-eastern corner of the North Island, facing the
Pacific, with its largest city of the same name and Hawke's Bay to the south.

21
00:03:25.432 --> 00:03:34.221 line:2% position:50%,center size:96% align:center
Captain Cook came ashore in New Zealand here in 1769, and the town was named
in 1870 for William Gisborne, a colonial official in London.

22
00:03:34.883 --> 00:03:41.570 line:2% position:50%,center size:96% align:center
Remember the place where the sun comes up among the first in the world, and
where Cook first set foot on New Zealand.

23
00:03:42.268 --> 00:03:44.439 line:2% position:50%,center size:96% align:center
Let's bring Taranaki into view.

24
00:03:50.205 --> 00:04:01.548 line:2% position:50%,center size:96% align:center
Taranaki is the small region on the west coast of the North Island, between
Waikato and Manawatū-Whanganui, with its largest city, New Plymouth, on the
shore.

25
00:04:02.208 --> 00:04:13.296 line:2% position:50%,center size:96% align:center
The mountain that gives the region its name, which Captain Cook renamed Mount
Egmont in 1770, is an almost perfect volcanic cone 2,518 metres high.

26
00:04:13.957 --> 00:04:20.435 line:2% position:50%,center size:96% align:center
Remember the perfect cone on the west coast, the one that doubled for Mount
Fuji in a film.

27
00:04:21.159 --> 00:04:23.458 line:2% position:50%,center size:96% align:center
Let's bring Hawke's Bay into view.

28
00:04:29.769 --> 00:04:40.392 line:2% position:50%,center size:96% align:center
Hawke's Bay is on the east coast of the North Island, with its largest city,
Hastings, inland from the shore, sitting south of Gisborne and north of the
Wellington region.

29
00:04:41.049 --> 00:04:49.791 line:2% position:50%,center size:96% align:center
Napier was destroyed by a strong earthquake in 1931 and rebuilt in the Art
Deco style, so the centre still looks like the 1930s.

30
00:04:50.454 --> 00:04:56.213 line:2% position:50%,center size:96% align:center
Remember the bay named for an admiral and the earthquake town rebuilt in deco
style.

31
00:04:56.915 --> 00:05:01.222 line:2% position:50%,center size:96% align:center
Let's bring Manawatū-Whanganui into view.

32
00:05:05.866 --> 00:05:22.066 line:2% position:50%,center size:96% align:center
Manawatū-Whanganui is the inland region in the middle of the North Island,
between Taranaki on the west and Hawke's Bay on the east, with its largest
city, Palmerston North, in the south and the snow-capped Mount Ruapehu in the
far south of it.

33
00:05:22.731 --> 00:05:34.678 line:2% position:50%,center size:96% align:center
In 2017 the Whanganui River was recognised in court as a living being, the
first river in the world to be given the legal rights of a person.

34
00:05:35.372 --> 00:05:41.978 line:2% position:50%,center size:96% align:center
Remember the region named after two rivers, one of which counts as a person in
law.

35
00:05:42.701 --> 00:05:44.826 line:2% position:50%,center size:96% align:center
Let's bring Wellington into view.

36
00:05:51.458 --> 00:06:03.567 line:2% position:50%,center size:96% align:center
Wellington is the region at the southern tip of the North Island, with the
capital city of the same name on its harbour and Manawatū-Whanganui to the
north.

37
00:06:04.251 --> 00:06:13.167 line:2% position:50%,center size:96% align:center
Te Papa, the national museum, stands on the waterfront, and its name comes
from the Māori words for the world of the ancestors.

38
00:06:13.828 --> 00:06:20.051 line:2% position:50%,center size:96% align:center
Remember the windy capital at the foot of the North Island, named for the duke
who won at Waterloo.

39
00:06:20.753 --> 00:06:22.622 line:2% position:50%,center size:96% align:center
Let's bring Nelson into view.

40
00:06:31.058 --> 00:06:41.553 line:2% position:50%,center size:96% align:center
Nelson is the small region at the top of the South Island, on the north coast
between Tasman and Marlborough, with its largest city of the same name at the
shore.

41
00:06:42.215 --> 00:06:52.966 line:2% position:50%,center size:96% align:center
It claims to be one of the sunniest places in New Zealand, and the World of
WearableArt, a show of costumes made as art, began here in 1987.

42
00:06:53.620 --> 00:07:00.273 line:2% position:50%,center size:96% align:center
Remember the sunny corner at the top of the South Island, named for the
admiral who died at Trafalgar.

43
00:07:00.998 --> 00:07:02.914 line:2% position:50%,center size:96% align:center
Let's bring Tasman into view.

44
00:07:10.537 --> 00:07:20.510 line:2% position:50%,center size:96% align:center
Tasman is the region across the top of the South Island, west of Nelson and
Marlborough, running down the coast to Canterbury, with no large city of its
own.

45
00:07:21.173 --> 00:07:33.538 line:2% position:50%,center size:96% align:center
It is named for Abel Tasman, the Dutch explorer who sighted New Zealand in
1642, and the long curve of Farewell Spit reaches out into the sea at its
northern corner.

46
00:07:34.192 --> 00:07:44.258 line:2% position:50%,center size:96% align:center
Remember the long sand spit sweeping into the sea at the top of the island,
named for the Dutch sailor who first sighted the country.

47
00:07:44.940 --> 00:07:47.065 line:2% position:50%,center size:96% align:center
Let's bring Marlborough into view.

48
00:07:52.513 --> 00:08:02.486 line:2% position:50%,center size:96% align:center
Marlborough is the region at the north-east of the South Island, with its
largest city, Blenheim, in the north and the maze of the Marlborough Sounds at
the top of the island.

49
00:08:03.138 --> 00:08:10.638 line:2% position:50%,center size:96% align:center
It is by far New Zealand's biggest wine region, and most of the sauvignon
blanc the country exports is grown here.

50
00:08:11.293 --> 00:08:17.261 line:2% position:50%,center size:96% align:center
Remember the drowned valleys of the Sounds and the region that grows the
country's wine.

51
00:08:17.984 --> 00:08:20.364 line:2% position:50%,center size:96% align:center
Let's bring West Coast into view.

52
00:08:26.383 --> 00:08:40.965 line:2% position:50%,center size:96% align:center
West Coast is the long thin region on the western side of the South Island,
running from Tasman in the north down to Fiordland in the south, with its
largest city, Greymouth, on the coast.

53
00:08:41.620 --> 00:08:53.137 line:2% position:50%,center size:96% align:center
The gold rush of the 1860s brought thousands of diggers to its rivers, and the
town of Charleston keeps the old miners' cottages on its main street.

54
00:08:53.794 --> 00:09:00.017 line:2% position:50%,center size:96% align:center
Remember the narrow western strip where the glaciers come down almost to the
rainforest by the sea.

55
00:09:00.718 --> 00:09:03.145 line:2% position:50%,center size:96% align:center
Let's bring Canterbury into view.

56
00:09:08.403 --> 00:09:21.687 line:2% position:50%,center size:96% align:center
Canterbury fills the middle of the east coast of the South Island, spreading
from the Marlborough border and the snowy Southern Alps down over the flat
plains to Otago, with its largest city, Christchurch, on the shore.

57
00:09:22.345 --> 00:09:33.560 line:2% position:50%,center size:96% align:center
Aoraki, or Mount Cook, 3,724 metres, the highest mountain in New Zealand,
rises in the Southern Alps at the head of the Canterbury region.

58
00:09:34.223 --> 00:09:41.305 line:2% position:50%,center size:96% align:center
Remember the plains and the snow peak behind them, and the city that was
rebuilt after the earthquakes.

59
00:09:42.078 --> 00:09:45.015 line:2% position:50%,center size:96% align:center
Let's bring Otago into view.

60
00:09:56.574 --> 00:10:08.648 line:2% position:50%,center size:96% align:center
Otago lies in the south-west of the South Island, between Canterbury and
Southland, reaching out to the Otago Peninsula and the city of Dunedin on the
east coast.

61
00:10:09.319 --> 00:10:21.939 line:2% position:50%,center size:96% align:center
Dunedin was founded in 1848 by Scottish Free Church settlers and was nicknamed
the Edinburgh of the South, and its university, founded in 1869, is the oldest
in the country.

62
00:10:22.601 --> 00:10:29.509 line:2% position:50%,center size:96% align:center
Remember the Scottish city of Dunedin, with albatrosses nesting at Taiaroa
Head on its peninsula.

63
00:10:30.268 --> 00:10:32.869 line:2% position:50%,center size:96% align:center
Let's bring Southland into view.

64
00:10:44.910 --> 00:10:58.262 line:2% position:50%,center size:96% align:center
Southland is the southernmost region of the South Island, below Otago and the
West Coast, with its largest city, Invercargill, in the south and Fiordland
along its western side.

65
00:10:58.928 --> 00:11:10.271 line:2% position:50%,center size:96% align:center
Fiordland, which lies largely within the region, holds Milford Sound, one of
the wettest places in the country, where waterfalls drop straight into the
sea.

66
00:11:10.929 --> 00:11:18.905 line:2% position:50%,center size:96% align:center
Remember the very bottom of the island, and the wild kiwi of Stewart Island,
or Rakiura, that can be seen in daylight.

67
00:11:29.665 --> 00:11:52.633 line:2% position:50%,center size:96% align:center
16 regions of New Zealand, one for each target of the map: Northland,
Auckland, Waikato, Bay of Plenty, Gisborne, Taranaki, Hawke's Bay,
Manawatū-Whanganui, Wellington, Nelson, Tasman, Marlborough, West Coast,
Canterbury, Otago and Southland.

68
00:11:53.288 --> 00:12:04.376 line:2% position:50%,center size:96% align:center
Remember the shape of New Zealand from the inside: the regions nest around
each other, and every name on the map belongs somewhere in this one country.
